WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:02.440
STEPHANIE WONG: So far
for our Hipster Shop app,

00:00:02.440 --> 00:00:05.530
we have Istio set up with our
Kubernetes engine cluster,

00:00:05.530 --> 00:00:08.140
and we've set up some
intelligent traffic management

00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:09.250
using Istio.

00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:11.084
But how do I monitor
each Istio component?

00:00:11.084 --> 00:00:13.000
KRISTIN AYALA: Well,
like many of our services

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:14.941
on Google Cloud,
Stackdriver can integrate

00:00:14.941 --> 00:00:17.440
and monitoring your Kubernetes
cluster and Istio components.

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:20.009
STEPHANIE WONG: Ah, Stackdriver,
another hipster favorite.

00:00:20.009 --> 00:00:20.800
KRISTIN AYALA: Yes.

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:23.228
Let's jump into it, and I'll
show you how it all looks.

00:00:23.228 --> 00:00:26.096
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:30.404 --> 00:00:32.320
KRISTIN AYALA: Stackdriver
combined with Istio

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:34.150
reduces the
complexity of managing

00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:35.860
micro-service
deployments by providing

00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:39.650
a uniform way to log, monitor,
and trace your application.

00:00:39.650 --> 00:00:41.350
Once the Istio
service is set up,

00:00:41.350 --> 00:00:44.200
we can first visit
the metrics explorer.

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:46.870
From here, type Istio
in the Find Resource

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:49.780
Type and Metric section.

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:52.060
Numerous additional metrics
from the Istio service

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:53.780
are now available.

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:57.400
Let's look at Client
Request Count.

00:00:57.400 --> 00:00:59.380
This metric shows the
number of client requests

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:00.970
sent by the service.

00:01:00.970 --> 00:01:02.350
On the right side,
we see a graph

00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:04.610
with numerous service
records and details

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:05.610
when we mouse over them.

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:10.680
On the left side,
we have filters

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:14.050
to narrow down or combine
data elements on the graph.

00:01:14.050 --> 00:01:15.690
Let's get a mean of
the client request

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:16.815
count for all the services.

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:23.990
Wow, that looks better.

00:01:23.990 --> 00:01:26.630
Not only are we able to
view individual metrics,

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:29.270
but with Istio, we are able
to see how our services are

00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:31.100
connected from a top-down view.

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:33.470
Let's visit the
topology section.

00:01:33.470 --> 00:01:34.940
If we mouse over
the front end, we

00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:38.150
are able to see specific
services it communicates with.

00:01:38.150 --> 00:01:40.400
Depending on the data made
available with the service,

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:42.830
things like latency
may also appear.

00:01:42.830 --> 00:01:45.470
This becomes especially useful
for large-scale projects

00:01:45.470 --> 00:01:47.700
with hundreds of
connected services.

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:50.600
Additionally, if you are curious
about how a particular service

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:55.130
performs, you can drill into
each section from the Overview.

00:01:55.130 --> 00:01:57.150
Here we can see the
service level specifics,

00:01:57.150 --> 00:02:01.460
including the individual request
count we looked at earlier.

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:03.110
Under the service
section, it gives us

00:02:03.110 --> 00:02:05.750
yet another view of
Istio data this time with

00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:08.030
a per service detailed
view of all the metrics.

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:13.620
Date and time filters are
provided at the top of the page

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:15.990
to narrow down the metrics
to exactly when you

00:02:15.990 --> 00:02:16.930
want to see your data.

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:22.530
Just below that,
there are three tabs--

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:25.370
SLOs, Metrics, and Diagnostics.

00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:29.500
The SLOs tab provides things
like compliance, indicators,

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:33.010
and errors to help assess if a
specific service is within SLO.

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:39.180
The next tab, Metrics,
provides the same details

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:43.500
we saw on the drill down
with the topology view.

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:45.900
The third tab,
Diagnostics, provides us

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:49.245
with service level, trace
data, errors, and logs.

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:55.720
Here we are able to
select filters and see

00:02:55.720 --> 00:02:57.910
which specific functions
within the service

00:02:57.910 --> 00:02:58.990
are slowing things down.

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:07.500
On the top, we can see the
parameters and values called

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:08.775
within the specific traces.

00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:16.260
On the right-hand
column, triggered events

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:17.850
from service level
policies appear.

00:03:20.790 --> 00:03:23.782
Each of these sections links
to respective Stackdriver page

00:03:23.782 --> 00:03:24.615
for further details.

00:03:27.890 --> 00:03:30.080
Here, we can see the
expanded trace lists

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:32.295
along with timing data
to get a better overview.

00:03:39.850 --> 00:03:41.800
Data doesn't always
look perfect.

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:43.630
To help test
configurations, we can

00:03:43.630 --> 00:03:45.340
use [INAUDIBLE]
files, such as this,

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:47.440
to mess up those pretty
graphs and see how

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:49.360
our application handles faults.

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:52.170
By calling this
file using Kube CTL,

00:03:52.170 --> 00:03:55.420
we are able to halt 50% of the
traffic in our shipping service

00:03:55.420 --> 00:03:57.190
by giving a 505 error.

00:03:57.190 --> 00:04:00.171
More details on fault injection
with Istio are linked below.

00:04:00.171 --> 00:04:02.170
STEPHANIE WONG: Seeing
that service graph really

00:04:02.170 --> 00:04:04.670
adds clarity to how each
Istio component interacts.

00:04:04.670 --> 00:04:05.170
I love it.

00:04:05.170 --> 00:04:07.336
KRISTIN AYALA: Yes, Stackdriver
makes it really easy

00:04:07.336 --> 00:04:09.625
to monitor, trace, and debug
your Istio applications.

00:04:09.625 --> 00:04:10.690
STEPHANIE WONG:
Well, I can't wait

00:04:10.690 --> 00:04:12.648
to see what else you have
in store to integrate

00:04:12.648 --> 00:04:14.890
with an Istio-enabled
Kubernetes cluster.

00:04:14.890 --> 00:04:17.290
Don't forget to like, comment,
and subscribe to stay up

00:04:17.290 --> 00:04:18.805
to date with our next episode.

00:04:18.805 --> 00:04:20.800
KRISTIN AYALA: And if you'd
like to get in contact with us,

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:22.479
click on the link
in the description.

00:04:22.479 --> 00:04:25.829
[MUSIC PLAYING]

